Expose the received final size of a stream.

RFC 9000 4.5 settles a stream's final size when a FIN or RESET_STREAM
arrives, and draft-ietf-webtrans-http3-16 5.4 requires it to implement
WT_MAX_DATA: without it the two endpoints disagree about how much
session credit a reset stream consumed. MsQuic already keeps the value
as Stream->RecvMaxLength, documented there as the immutable final size,
but PEER_SEND_ABORTED carries only an error code and the statistics
struct has no byte counts, so there is no public way to read it.

Google quiche (QuicRstStreamFrame::byte_offset), Cloudflare quiche
(RecvBuf::reset returning final_size - len) and aioquic (final_size in
_handle_reset_stream_frame) all surface it for the same reason.

Read-only accessor; no state changes. QUIC_STATUS_INVALID_STATE while
the final size is still unknown, matching how 0RTT_LENGTH reports a
value that is not yet settled.

The identifier is deliberately 0x08000046 rather than the next free
0x08000006. Upstream allocates stream parameters sequentially and has no
reserved vendor range, so the next number MsQuic itself adds would
collide with ours. Sitting well above the sequence keeps an MsQuic
upgrade from quietly meaning something else. A collision would still be
a compile error, not silent, but it is cheaper to avoid it.
Renumber to the next sequential value if this is ever upstreamed.

Note: MsQuic PR branch 37ea3bcaf ("Added final size to
QuicStreamProcessReliableResetFrame") touches related internals and is
not merged. Check it before submitting this upstream.


